  "market_commentary": {
    "overview": "Sale & Purchase: The second-hand market remained active, with a further forty-four vessels reported sold \nacross all sectors (20 bulkers, 15 tankers, 7 gas carriers and 2 containerships), and values held firm despite \nthe sharp correction in tanker freight rates that followed the US mid-term elections. Buying interest remained \nstrongest for geared tonnage and for Panamaxes from Far Eastern buyers, while appetite for Capesizes \nappeared slightly more subdued.  \nIn the bulker sector, 20 vessels changed hands: 2 Capesizes, 2 Post-Panamaxes, 2 Kamsarmaxes, 1 Panamax, \n1 Ultramax, 4 Supramaxes and 8 Handysizes. Clients of Seanergy Maritime of Greece paid region low USD 70 \nmill for the 2019-built eco Capesize NIGHTSKY (183,017 dwt, Namura/Japan) from compatriots Alcyon \nShipping, basis delivery in January 2027. Further down the age scale, the scrubber-fitted PAN ADVANCE \n(179,185 dwt, 2009 Hyundai/Korea) went to clients of Lila Global for region USD 35.4 - 35.8 mill, also basis \ndelivery in January 2027, in line with the USD 35 - 36.5 mill paid last week for the two-year-older Setouchi-\nmax CAPE ALLIANCE (206,190 dwt, 2007 Imabari/Japan).  \nIn the Post-Panamax segment, the INDUS TRIUMPH (92,967 dwt, 2012 Taizhou Sanfu/China) also went to \nGreek buyers for region USD 16.5 - 16.6 mill, in line with the USD 16.5 mill paid for the one-year-older CARL \nOLDENDORFF (93,028 dwt, 2011 Taizhou CATIC/China) two weeks ago, while the 2010 Yangfan-built SHUN \nCHANG (93,322 dwt) achieved USD 15 mill.  \nIn the Kamsarmax segment, the 2019 Oshima-built TAHO AUSTRALIA (81,788 dwt), for which offers were \ninvited on 6th November 2026, is rumoured to be trading at region USD 38 mill and the 2012 Jinhai-built \nZHEN NING (79,463 dwt) achieved USD 20.39 mill, while Chinese buyers paid region USD 9 mill basis prompt \ndelivery Turkey for the 25-year-old Japanese Panamax TARIEL (76,623 dwt, 2001 Imabari/Japan).  \nIn the geared sizes, the 2013 Shin Kasado-built Ultramax DIAMOND QUEEN (61,414 dwt) achieved region \nUSD 24 mill, while the 25-year-old Supramax YIN HE HAI NAN DAO (48,983 dwt, 2001 IHI/Japan) was sold at \nonline auction for USD 7.94 mill. In between, the VINCA (52,447 dwt, 2006 Tsuneishi Cebu/Philippines) \nfetched USD 12.5 mill basis delivery in January 2027, a firm level against the USD 9 mill paid last week for the \nthree-year-older HV ALICE (52,425 dwt, 2003) from the same yard. Chinese-built Supramaxes also changed \nhands, with the TONY SMITH (56,498 dwt, 2011 Qingshan/China) fetching excess USD 15.5 mill and the older \nKANG CHENG (55,541 dwt, 2004 Nantong COSCO KHI/China) USD 11.55 mill. \nIn the Handysize segment, the 2017 Shimanami-built ICY BAY (37,570 dwt), for which best offers were invited \non 4th November 2026, is reported sold at region USD 24 mill. That level stands about USD 0.5 mill above \nthe USD 23.5 mill paid three weeks ago for the one-year-older, Naikai-built OHBS MOUNTPARK (37,839 dwt, \n2016) and some USD 2 mill above the USD 22 mill achieved in September by the one-year-older, Shimanami-\nbuilt ULTRA TATIO (37,927 dwt, 2016), underlining the continued firming of modern Japanese Handysize \nvalues. Similarly, the smaller 2016 Namura-built AFRICAN OWL (34,382 dwt) is understood to have received \noffers between USD 20 - 21 mill.  \nAt the larger end of the segment, the 2022-built DANAE (40,015 dwt, Shin Kurushima/Japan) was sold for \nUSD 34.5 mill, while the 2017 Nanjing Dongze-built sisters JINLING CONFIDENCE and NANJING CONFIDENCE \n(40,320 dwt & 40,266 dwt) achieved USD 24 mill each. Among older units, the Shimanami-built FUGA (38,036 \ndwt, 2012) went to Vietnamese buyers for low USD 19 mill basis prompt delivery. The open-hatch, box-\nshaped EFFICIENCY OL (37,130 dwt, 2010 Saiki/Japan) fetched region USD 14.7 - 15.35 mill, in line with the \nregion USD 14.5 mill rumoured last week for the conventional LUCKY VOYAGER (37,317 dwt, 2009 \nSaiki/Japan), while the ice-classed AURELIA (37,294 dwt, 2012 Zhejiang Ouhua/China) is rumoured \ncommitted at region high USD 13 - 14 mill and the COREOCEAN OL (28,358 dwt, 2009 Imabari/Japan) USD \n9.2 mill. \nOn the tanker front, 15 sales were reported. Clients of ADNOC acquired two units from Trafigura: the 2017-\nbuilt VLCC OLYMPIC LADY (approx. 299,000 dwt, Hyundai Gunsan/Korea) for USD 185 mill and the Suezmax \nSPONDE (158,500 dwt, 2012 Samsung/Korea) for USD 110 mill, matching last week's sale of the same-age \nFLEVES (158,517 dwt, 2012 Hyundai/Korea). Trafigura is understood to have realised a gain of about USD 36 \nmill on the SPONDE, having bought her for USD 74 mill in September. ADNOC was also behind the purchase \nof the non-eco VLCC EUROSPIRIT (320,105 dwt, 2011 Daewoo/Korea, SS/DD due 11/2026) for USD 153 mill, \na vessel her sellers had acquired for just USD 48 mill in 2020.  \nIn the MR segment, four methanol-ready Qidong Qianyao resales (49,800 dwt, 2027 - 2028 delivery) were \nsold at USD 50 mill each, slightly below the USD 53 - 55 mill reported for the four Chengxi-built CS units last \nweek. In the smaller sizes, the stainless steel TARUCA (19,983 dwt, 2005 Fukuoka/Japan) was sold for region \nmid-high USD 12 mill.  \nThe gas sector provided the headline deal of the week, with Mitsui OSK selling four ice-class 170,000 cbm \nLNG carriers, the NORTH VALLEY, NORTH LIGHT, NORTH OCEAN and NORTH MOON (2024 - 2025 \nDaewoo/Korea), to Novatek Gas & Power for USD 897.5 mill en bloc, or about USD 224 mill each. Clients of \nSingapore-based Sinogas Maritime acquired the 155,000 cbm tri-fuel diesel-electric GASLOG SHANGHAI and \nGASLOG SALEM (2013 & 2015 Samsung/Korea), both damaged in separate drone attacks in the Middle East \nearlier this year, at online auction for region USD 100 mill each, and Chinese buyers paid USD 80 mill for the \nVLGC FRITZI N (80,676 cbm, 2009 Hyundai/Korea), just below the USD 81 mill paid last week for the one-\nyear-older GAS WARICH (82,026 cbm, 2008 DSME/Korea).  \nFor a detailed review and additional information regarding this week's transactions, kindly refer to our \n\"representative sales\" section.",
    "demolition": "The recycling market remained starved of tonnage, with owners continuing to keep older vessels \ntrading in the firm freight markets and most of the week's limited business involving small dry units and \nsanctioned tonnage. Bangladesh remained the strongest destination and firmed further, with buyers keen \nfor candidates at around USD 540 - 550 per LT for bulkers and USD 560 per LT for tankers; the small 1991-\nbuilt cement carrier SHINAKI MARU (2,621 ldt) was sold there at USD 460 per LT. India firmed, with recyclers' \nsentiment positive despite softer local steel prices during the Diwali holidays, and levels improved to around \nUSD 470 - 480 per LT for bulkers and USD 490 - 495 per LT for tankers. Pakistan remained subdued, with \nample steel supply limiting recyclers' appetite, while Turkey held steady, with damaged candidates in the \nBlack Sea difficult to tow to Aliaga. \nFor an overview of demolition market rates, please check our comprehensive demometer below:"
  },
